The Linux Foundation estimates open source software provides $8.8 trillion in value if organisations had to develop equivalent proprietary software — the cost saving to the global economy is enormous.
Developing countries and small businesses that cannot afford enterprise software licenses run on open source — LibreOffice, Linux, and LAMP stacks enable economic participation that expensive proprietary alternatives would exclude.
CERN, the European Space Agency, and the US Department of Defense have all mandated open source for critical systems — not for ideological reasons but because it is more cost-effective and secure.
